  <h1>Puppy nutrition: the first year decides the next ten</h1>
  <p class="guide-lede">Bones, joints, gut, coat, even temperament around food — all of it is being built right now, out of whatever's in the bowl. Puppy feeding isn't complicated, but the few rules that exist really matter.</p>

  <h2>Does my puppy actually need puppy food?</h2>
  <p>Yes — this one isn't marketing. Growing bodies need more protein, more fat, and different calcium-to-phosphorus ratios than adult maintenance food provides. Feed a food formulated for growth until roughly 12 months for most dogs, longer for giant breeds — then transition off it, because puppy-food calories on an adult dog is how the weight problem starts.</p>

  <h2>What's different for large breeds?</h2>
  <p>Large and giant breed puppies need <em>controlled</em> growth — excess calcium and calories during the growth window are linked to developmental joint problems in the breeds already prone to them. That's why "large-breed puppy" formulas exist and why free-feeding a Great Dane puppy is a genuinely bad idea. If your dog will finish over ~50 pounds, feed a large-breed growth formula and keep them lean while they grow.</p>

  <h2>How often should a puppy eat?</h2>
  <p>Small stomachs, big needs: three to four meals a day until about six months, then two. Scheduled meals beat free-feeding — you see appetite changes immediately (the earliest sickness signal you'll get), and housetraining is dramatically easier when input is on a schedule.</p>

  <h2>What about treats during training?</h2>
  <p>Puppyhood is peak treat season, and treats are unbudgeted calories. Keep training treats tiny — pea-sized, low-calorie — or use pieces of the regular kibble ration. A puppy will work just as hard for it, and you're not building a chubby adolescent while you build a sit.</p>

  <h2>What should be on the panel?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A named protein first</strong> — growth is built from protein, so its source is the headline.</li>
    <li><strong>Omega-3s (DHA)</strong> — supports the developing brain and eyes; the better growth formulas include it.</li>
    <li><strong>A statement that it's formulated for growth</strong> (or all life stages) — that's the line that carries the calcium/phosphorus and calorie guarantees.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When is it a vet problem?</h2>
  <p>A puppy who won't eat for more than a meal or two, diarrhea beyond a day, a pot belly with a thin frame, or any limping during growth — puppies crash faster than adults, so call earlier than you would for a grown dog. And bring your food questions to the puppy visits; you're paying for them anyway.</p>

